Let $B$ be a contraction and let $U,V$ be compatible deterministic matrices. At the almost-everywhere boundary values of the rational function in question, $$\left\|\left\lVert U(\mathbf 1-zB)^{-1}V\right\rVert_{\mathrm{HS}}\right\|_{L^{1,\infty}}
       \le C\left\lVert U\right\rVert_{\mathrm{HS}}\left\lVert V\right\rVert_{\mathrm{HS}}.
 \label{mat:weak-sandwich}$$ For any compatible square matrix $D$, $$\left\|\left\lvert\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}\bigl(D(\mathbf 1-zB)^{-1}\bigr)\right\rvert\right\|_{L^{1,\infty}}
       \le C\left\lVert D\right\rVert_1.
 \label{mat:weak-trace}$$ Here and below $\left\lVert D\right\rVert_1$ denotes the trace norm. In particular, every moment of order $0<p<1$ is bounded by $C_p$ times the corresponding right-hand scale to the power $p$.

Let $I$ be an interval of length at least $\ell$, let $N$ be a Hilbert-space-valued polynomial of fixed degree on $I$, and let $z$ be a continuously differentiable complex function satisfying $$\left\lvert z(s)\right\rvert\ge c_0 a,
 \qquad \left\lvert z'(s)\right\rvert\le C_0,
 \qquad a>0.$$ Then $$\sup_{s\in I}\frac{\left\lVert N(s)\right\rVert^2}{\left\lvert z(s)\right\rvert^2}
 \le \frac{C}{\min(\ell,a)}
       \int_I\frac{\left\lVert N(s)\right\rVert^2}{\left\lvert z(s)\right\rvert^2}\,\,\mathrm ds.$$ Here $C$ depends only on $c_0,C_0$ and the degree bound.

For suitable fixed $d_0,\gamma>0$, $\mathbb P(\mathcal B^c)\leq C e^{-cM_0R}$. On $\mathcal B\cap\mathcal P$, for every compatible matrix $D'$ on the source columns indexed by $J$, $$\label{van:barrier-bound}
 \left\lVert A_{IJ}D'\right\rVert_{\mathrm{HS}}
 \leq \frac{C}{M_0R\tau d_0}
       \left\lVert S_{CJ}D'\right\rVert_{\mathrm{HS}}.$$ The constant includes the fixed factor $\gamma^{-1}$. The same cutoff argument controls every absorbed output supported where the cutoff equals one; it requires no norm bound on the exterior resolvent.

Assume $d>0$. There is a constant $c_1>0$ with the following property. For every sufficiently small fixed $\kappa>0$, one can choose $M_0$ sufficiently large and finite constants $C_1,C_F$, before choosing $C_0,n,m,N$, so that the following holds. For any fixed sufficiently large $C_0$, and then $n,m,N$ sufficiently large successively, an event $\mathcal G$ of probability at least $1/2$ satisfies $$\begin{gathered}
 \mathcal G\subset\mathcal P\cap\mathcal B,\qquad
 K\leq\kappa,\label{van:good-transmission}\\
 c_1d\leq
 \sum_{i,j\in I}|F(i)-F(j)|^2|A_{ij}|^2
 \leq C_1,\label{van:good-jumps}\\
 \left\lVert F_\nu T\right\rVert_{\mathrm{HS}}\leq C_F
 \quad (1\leq\nu\leq4).\label{van:good-weighted}
\end{gathered}$$ In particular, on this event, $$\label{van:external-small}
 \left\lVert A_{IJ}\right\rVert_{\mathrm{HS}}
 +\sum_{\nu=1}^4\left\lVert A_{IJ}F_\nu|_J\right\rVert_{\mathrm{HS}}
 \leq \frac{C(\sqrt\kappa+C_F)}{M_0C_0d_0}.$$ The right side tends to zero as $C_0$ increases, with all constants chosen before it kept fixed.

Choose $\kappa$ sufficiently small depending on $d$, then the constants in <a href="#van:good" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="van:good">34</a>, and then $C_0$ sufficiently large. There are constants $c_2>0$ and $B_p<\infty$, independent of the later choices $n,m,N$, such that on $\mathcal G$ $$\label{van:central-bound}
 |\mathfrak h(0)|\geq c_2,$$ and, for every fixed $0<p<1/2$, $$\label{van:hardy-bound}
 \int_{\mathbb T}|H(z)|^p\,\,\mathrm d\gamma_{\mathbb T}(z)\leq B_p.$$ Here $\gamma_{\mathbb T}$ is normalized Haar measure. Neither constant depends on the number of cell ports or probes.

Fix $0<s<1/16$. There are constants $A=A(h,E,s)<\infty$, $a=a(h,E,s)>0$, and an integer $N_0=N_0(h,E,s)$ such that, for every $N\ge N_0$ and every $x,y\in\mathbb T_N^2$, $$\label{boot:real-decay}
 \mathbb E\left\lvert(H_N-E)^{-1}_{xy}\right\rvert^{s/2}
       \le A\exp\{-a\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,y)\}.$$ Here $H_N$ has the true iid uniform $[-h,h]$ diagonal and periodic nearest-neighbor hopping. Its inverse at the specified real energy exists almost surely. No uniformity in $E$, nor any statement about time evolution, is part of this theorem.

There is $C_s<\infty$, independent of the masks, centers, $R,N$, and $C_0\ge1$, such that $$\label{boot:twist-ineq}
 \mathbb E\left\lVert S^{\rm old}_{Y'X}\right\rVert_{\rm HS}^{s}
     \le C_s(1+C_0)\mathbb E\bigl[D^s+V^sU^s\bigr].$$ As everywhere in this section, the constant may depend on the fixed $h,E,s$ and profile.

Suppose $\mathbb EV^s>0$, and define the probability law $$\,\mathrm d\nu=\frac{V^s}{\mathbb EV^s}\,\,\mathrm d\mathbb P.$$ For a site $i\in C$, conditional on all other potentials, put $W(v_i)=V^s$. Then $$\label{boot:l2l1}
       \bigl(\mathbb E_i W^2\bigr)^{1/2}\le K_s\mathbb E_i W,$$ with a constant uniform in every frozen exterior and in the mask. For $d_0>0$ sufficiently small, chosen after $s$ and before $C_0$, there are $K,c>0$ such that $$\label{boot:tilted-barriers}
 \mathbb P(\mathcal B^c)+\nu(\mathcal B^c)\le K e^{-cR},
 \qquad
 \mathbb E_\nu d_{\min}^{-2s}\le K R^2,
 \qquad d_{\min}=\min_{i\in C}\vartheta(v_i).$$ No independence under $\nu$ is asserted or needed.
